<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n17423" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">exulcĕrātĭo</span></b> ,
			ōnis, f.
		exulcero, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a soreness</i>, <i>festering</i>, <i>exulceration</i> (post-Aug.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">si nulla exulceratio est,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cels.
					4, 22</span></i>
			.—In plur.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vesicarum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					20, 3, 8, § 17</span></i>
			.—* </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: verebar, ne haec non consolatio sed
			exulceratio esset, i. e. <i>exasperation</i>, <i>aggravation</i> of pain, <i><span class="en">Sen. Cons. ad Helv. 1, 4</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
